WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:01.941
I'm Ninebark.

00:00:01.941 --> 00:00:05.197
How fast can two n by n matrices be multiplied?

00:00:05.197 --> 00:00:08.778
OpenAI now says: n to the nine fourths, and no faster.

00:00:11.242 --> 00:00:14.212
Strassen used seven products where the schoolbook uses eight.

00:00:14.212 --> 00:00:18.173
Recursion gave two point eight oh seven, and by nineteen ninety the

00:00:18.173 --> 00:00:20.483
bound was two point three seven five.

00:00:20.903 --> 00:00:23.928
Then thirty-six years of work on one tensor moved it by

00:00:23.928 --> 00:00:25.028
less than a hundredth.

00:00:25.448 --> 00:00:28.572
In October, OpenAI's paper dropped it to nine fourths.

00:00:28.572 --> 00:00:31.696
Six groups of human authors have reproved that since.

00:00:32.116 --> 00:00:33.769
The lower bound never left two.

00:00:33.769 --> 00:00:37.076
The new paper claims it rises all the way to nine fourths.

00:00:38.858 --> 00:00:40.689
The upper bound is settled.

00:00:40.689 --> 00:00:43.986
Six independent write-ups, one of them an explicit algorithm.

00:00:44.406 --> 00:00:47.985
The lower bound is one long preprint, and nobody outside OpenAI

00:00:47.985 --> 00:00:49.286
has explained it yet.

00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:52.460
The proof starts from a picture.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:55.533
Three factors at three sites, and each index is a wire

00:00:55.533 --> 00:00:56.651
between two of them.

00:00:57.031 --> 00:01:01.481
A rank decomposition replaces the wires with one label that all three share.

00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:03.691
Here is the formula.

00:01:04.567 --> 00:01:06.074
Give every wire a length.

00:01:06.074 --> 00:01:07.582
The tensor costs the perimeter.

00:01:08.002 --> 00:01:11.022
In flat space the shared label costs half the perimeter, and

00:01:11.022 --> 00:01:12.120
the bound is two.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.042
On a tripod it is nine fourths.

00:01:15.867 --> 00:01:20.257
If matrices multiplied faster than nine fourths, the paper shows tensor networks

00:01:20.257 --> 00:01:22.452
could be compressed again and again.

00:01:22.832 --> 00:01:26.186
Then checking lists for repeated entries would get too cheap, which

00:01:26.186 --> 00:01:27.405
a counting bound forbids.

00:01:29.067 --> 00:01:33.313
The exact exponent is stated against plain Mathlib and proved with standard

00:01:33.313 --> 00:01:35.789
axioms, and one outside builder reproduced it.

00:01:35.789 --> 00:01:38.620
The per-size gate count is not in Lean.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:41.283
Omega equals 9/4, every field.

00:01:41.971 --> 00:01:43.362
Alpha equals 1/2.

00:01:44.457 --> 00:01:45.869
Not the gate count per size.

00:01:48.558 --> 00:01:54.115
Clément Canonne, on Bluesky says: Common sense is dead, ω=2.25.

00:01:55.558 --> 00:01:57.032
The top is settled.

00:01:57.032 --> 00:01:58.507
The floor is machine-checked.

00:01:58.507 --> 00:02:01.087
Nobody outside OpenAI has explained why yet.

00:02:02.567 --> 00:02:05.972
Nine fourths from above, nine fourths from below, and a proof still

00:02:05.972 --> 00:02:07.391
waiting for a human reader.

00:02:07.811 --> 00:02:09.920
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:10.523
I'm Ninebark.

00:02:10.523 --> 00:02:10.825
Bye!
